# NOBORU ARCHITECTURE RULE

Version: 1.0

Priority: CRITICAL

Status: AUTHORITATIVE

This rule governs all architectural decisions within the Noboru codebase.

Every feature, service, module, component, and database interaction must follow this document.

If another rule conflicts with this rule, this rule takes precedence unless overridden by docs/MASTER_PROMPT.md.

⸻

PURPOSE

The purpose of this rule is to ensure:

* Scalability
* Maintainability
* Reliability
* Predictability
* Testability
* Long-term sustainability

Noboru is expected to grow for years.

Architecture must support long-term evolution.

⸻

PRIME DIRECTIVE

Build systems.

Not pages.

Build domains.

Not features.

Build reusable architecture.

Not temporary implementations.

Every implementation must fit into a larger system.

⸻

ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLES

Principle 1

Separation of Concerns

Each module owns one responsibility.

⸻

Principle 2

Single Source of Truth

Data should only exist in one authoritative location.

⸻

Principle 3

Domain Ownership

Every domain owns its own logic.

⸻

Principle 4

Composition Over Duplication

Reuse systems.

Never copy systems.

⸻

Principle 5

Scalable First

Design for growth.

Not current size.

⸻

Principle 6

Feature Isolation

Features should not directly depend on each other.

⸻

Principle 7

Offline First

All core learning systems must support offline operation.

⸻

APPLICATION LAYERS

The application must follow layered architecture.

⸻

UI Layer

Responsibilities:

* Presentation
* User interaction
* Visual feedback

Must NOT:

* Access database directly
* Contain business logic

⸻

Application Layer

Responsibilities:

* User workflows
* Feature orchestration

Must NOT:

* Perform database operations directly

⸻

Service Layer

Responsibilities:

* Business logic
* Rules
* Validation

Must NOT:

* Render UI

⸻

Repository Layer

Responsibilities:

* Data access
* Queries
* Persistence

Must NOT:

* Contain business logic

⸻

Database Layer

Responsibilities:

* Storage
* Relationships
* Security

Must NOT:

* Contain frontend concerns

⸻

FEATURE ARCHITECTURE

Every feature must be self-contained.

Required structure:

/features
feature-name/
components/
hooks/
services/
repositories/
types/
constants/
utils/
tests/
docs/

No feature may bypass this structure.

⸻

FEATURE OWNERSHIP

Each feature owns:

UI

Services

Types

Tests

Documentation

Dependencies

⸻

REQUIRED FEATURE MODULES

Authentication

Profile

Learning

Vocabulary

Kanji

Grammar

Review

Achievements

Leagues

Games

Community

Shop

Settings

Notifications

Admin

Analytics

⸻

SHARED MODULES

Shared functionality belongs in:

/lib

Only place reusable systems there.

⸻

SERVICE ARCHITECTURE

Business logic belongs in services.

Example:

services/
vocabulary.service.ts
kanji.service.ts
achievement.service.ts
review.service.ts

Services must be reusable.

Services must be testable.

⸻

REPOSITORY ARCHITECTURE

All database access belongs in repositories.

Example:

repositories/
vocabulary.repository.ts
kanji.repository.ts

Never query database directly from:

* Components
* Hooks
* Pages

⸻

DATABASE ACCESS RULE

Forbidden:

const data = await supabase
  .from("users")

Inside:

* Components
* Pages
* UI logic

Required:

Component

↓

Service

↓

Repository

↓

Database

⸻

STATE MANAGEMENT

Use the smallest possible scope.

Priority:

Local State

↓

Feature State

↓

Global State

Global state should be rare.

⸻

API RULES

Every endpoint must:

Validate input

Validate permissions

Return typed responses

Handle errors

Log failures

⸻

API STRUCTURE

/api
feature/
route.ts

Group by domain.

Not by HTTP method.

⸻

TYPE SAFETY

TypeScript strict mode required.

Forbidden:

any

Allowed only with explicit justification.

⸻

DOMAIN MODELS

Every domain requires:

Types

Schemas

Validation

Documentation

Tests

⸻

ERROR HANDLING

Every feature must support:

Loading State

Success State

Error State

Recovery State

Offline State

⸻

OFFLINE FIRST ARCHITECTURE

Core learning features must function offline.

Required:

Vocabulary

Kanji

Grammar

Reviews

Reading

Downloaded Lessons

Progress Tracking

Achievements

⸻

OFFLINE STORAGE

Use:

IndexedDB

Local Cache

Background Sync

Conflict Resolution

⸻

EDUCATIONAL DOMAIN RULE

Educational content is sacred.

Never hardcode educational content into UI.

Content must originate from:

Database

CMS

Content Systems

⸻

CONTENT OWNERSHIP

Vocabulary owns vocabulary data.

Kanji owns kanji data.

Grammar owns grammar data.

Lessons assemble content.

Lessons do not own content.

⸻

GAMIFICATION RULE

Gamification may not own educational progress.

Educational progress is authoritative.

Gamification reads educational progress.

Never the reverse.

⸻

PERFORMANCE RULES

Targets:

Initial Load < 2 Seconds

Route Change < 300ms

User Action < 100ms

Animation 60fps

Lighthouse > 90

⸻

SECURITY RULES

Every user-owned resource:

Requires RLS

Requires authorization

Requires ownership validation

⸻

DEPENDENCY RULES

Allowed:

UI → Services

Services → Repositories

Repositories → Database

Forbidden:

UI → Database

UI → Repository

Database → UI

⸻

TESTING REQUIREMENTS

Every feature requires:

Unit Tests

Integration Tests

Documentation

Error Handling

Type Coverage

⸻

DOCUMENTATION REQUIREMENTS

Every module requires:

Purpose

Responsibilities

Dependencies

Usage

Examples

Known Limitations

⸻

MCP ARCHITECTURE

MCP integrations must be isolated.

Required:

/services/mcp/

All MCP integrations belong here.

No feature may directly depend on MCP.

⸻

ASSET PIPELINE ARCHITECTURE

Assets are managed as a system.

Assets must:

Have metadata

Have ownership

Have versioning

Have registry entries

⸻

FUTURE AI RULE

Future local AI support must be abstracted.

Required:

/services/ai/

Must expose interfaces only.

No implementation required.

Application must function without AI.

⸻

FORBIDDEN ARCHITECTURE PATTERNS

Database queries inside components

Business logic inside pages

Business logic inside hooks

Duplicated services

Duplicated types

Circular dependencies

Untyped responses

Feature coupling

Hardcoded educational content

Direct MCP dependencies

Direct AI dependencies

⸻

ARCHITECTURAL SUCCESS CRITERIA

A new developer should be able to:

Understand project structure quickly.

Locate ownership quickly.

Add features safely.

Refactor safely.

Scale systems safely.

⸻

ARCHITECTURAL FAILURE CRITERIA

If any of the following occur:

Repeated code

Feature coupling

Hidden dependencies

Direct database access from UI

Unclear ownership

Large fragile modules

Undocumented systems

Then architecture is considered degraded.

Refactoring becomes mandatory.

⸻

NOBORU ARCHITECTURE PRINCIPLE

Every system should feel like a well-built mountain trail.

Clear.

Reliable.

Scalable.

Maintainable.

Easy to follow.

Difficult to get lost.

The codebase should guide developers upward just as Noboru guides learners upward.

